"""`aspect setup workspace-data` — print this build's commit, branch, PR and CI
metadata as Bazel workspace status, for `--workspace_status_command`.

    bazel build //... --workspace_status_command="aspect setup workspace-data"

Bazel takes one status command, so a repo that already has a status script of
its own calls this from inside that script rather than replacing it:

    #!/usr/bin/env bash
    echo "MY_OWN_KEY $(...)"
    aspect setup workspace-data || true

The `|| true` covers what this task cannot: it exits 0 by design, but an
`aspect` missing from PATH fails before the task runs, and under `set -e` that
would take the whole script — and with it the build — down. Both sets of lines
land in the same `volatile-status.txt`, so pick keys that do not collide with
the ones this prints (`bazel/build_metadata.axl`).

Bazel runs the command itself at the start of every invocation and reads `KEY
value` lines from its stdout, so the values are computed per build rather than
written somewhere and left to go stale. That is what makes this safe where a
file is not: the flag naming the command is identical for every job, commit and
workspace, so it can sit in a shared `~/.bazelrc` and reach every vanilla
`bazel` call on a CI runner — including sibling workspaces a repository's own
`.bazelrc` cannot reach — while the data behind it is never shared between two
builds.

The keys are the same ones `aspect <task>` sends as `--build_metadata`
(`bazel/build_metadata.axl`), and the Aspect Web UI merges the two sources,
`--build_metadata` winning on a clash (`lib/bazel_results.axl::merged_meta`).
So a vanilla `bazel` reaches the same Web UI fields — commit, branch, PR,
actor, repository, run URL — that a task does.

Keys are written unprefixed, which puts them in `volatile-status.txt`. A
`STABLE_` key invalidates every stamped action whenever it changes, and
attribution data changes every commit, so stamping is the wrong trade here.

Bazel fails the build outright when the status command exits non-zero, so this
task reports what it can and exits 0 — with no git, no `.aspect/` and no
workspace it prints only what the environment gave it. That covers the sibling
workspaces the shared-rc deployment reaches, since they carry no Aspect project
of their own. What it cannot cover is a failure before the task runs: a
workspace whose own `.aspect/config.axl` fails to evaluate, or an `aspect`
missing from PATH, takes every vanilla `bazel` call in that workspace down with
it. Both are conditions under which `aspect <task>` is already broken there.

Output discipline: the `KEY value` lines are a protocol payload Bazel parses,
so stdout carries them and nothing else — every narrative line this CLI prints
already goes to stderr, which Bazel leaves alone. A write error other than a
closed pipe propagates; a closed pipe means Bazel is no longer reading, and
there is nobody left to tell.
"""

load("@aspect//bazel/build_metadata.axl", "get_build_metadata", "workspace_status_lines")

def _workspace_data_impl(ctx: TaskContext) -> int:
    lines = workspace_status_lines(get_build_metadata(ctx.std))
    if lines:
        ctx.std.io.stdout.write("\n".join(lines) + "\n")
        ctx.std.io.stdout.flush()
    return 0

workspace_data = task(
    group = ["setup"],
    summary = "Print this build's commit, branch, PR and CI metadata as Bazel workspace status.",
    description = "Print the current commit, branch, pull request, actor, repository and CI run as Bazel workspace status — `KEY value` lines on stdout, for `bazel --workspace_status_command=\"aspect setup workspace-data\"`. Bazel runs it at the start of every invocation, so the values are always this build's; pointing at it from a shared rc is what lets a vanilla `bazel` in any workspace on a CI runner reach the Aspect Web UI with full attribution, without any per-build file to go stale. The keys match the `--build_metadata` an `aspect <task>` sends, which the Web UI merges with these. They are unprefixed, so they land in volatile-status.txt and never invalidate a stamped action. Prints nothing and exits 0 where there is no metadata to report.",
    implementation = _workspace_data_impl,
)
